These are great changes IMO! As you noted, @bam241, requiring use of a `std::shared_ptr` for the MOAB interface makes DAGMC a little more rigid. 

One thought might be to have a raw pointer to a `moab::Interface` in the DAGMC class which is set regardless of the constructor used and be returned by the `DAGMC::moab_instance()` method. Our constructors taking a raw `moab::Interface` pointer would then look like this:

```cpp
DagMC::DagMC(Interface* mb_impl, double overlap_tolerance, double p_numerical_precision) {
  // if we arent handed a moab instance, create one
  if (nullptr == mb_impl) {
   mb_shared = std::shared_ptr<Core>();
   MBI = mb_shared.get();
  } else {
   MBI = mb_impl;
  }
  
  // make new GeomTopoTool and GeomQueryTool
  ...
}
```

If the pointer is passed in, we won't delete it on cleanup but if we did create it, the `std::shared_ptr` will take care of itself when the `DagMC` object is destroyed. No `new`'s /`delete`'s to worry about on our end.

Does that sound plausible or am I missing something?

Clarification:

In the example above `MBI` is still our raw `moab::Interface*` and we would add an attribute called `mb_shared` or something similar for the `shared_ptr`.

this last comment re-add the constructor with a **Instance*** as an argument. This constructor is deprecated and only exist for backward compatibility.

the `DagMC` class will have 2 members for MBI, one `Instance* MBI` and a `share_ptr<Instance> MBI_shared_ptr` to avoid segaulting when deallocating the `DagMC` instance.

Indeed, having a constructor with a `Interface*` argument will induce pointer owner ship issue.
in the `Instance*` is directly casted into the `shared_ptr` taking the ownership on the pointer then at destruction of the `DagMC` object the `shared_ptr` will also be delete and deallocating the `Interface*` address... which may potentially lead to segfault.

With this construct the following case are possible:
- external `Interface*` (using the deprecated constructor) -> no shared_ptr is defined, the Instance* is owned outside of the DagMC object, so no need to delete it.
```
  Interface* mbi = new Interface();
  DagMC *dagmc = new DagMC(mbi);
  delete dagmc;
  delete mbi;
```


- external `shared_ptr< Interface >`, the chain of shared_ptr owns the address of the Instance and will deal with the deallocation when needed. (no need to delete the Interface*)
```
  shared_ptr<Interface> mbi = make_shared<Interface>();
  DagMC *dagmc = new DagMC(mbi);
  delete dagmc;
```

- internal definition of the Interface, the Interface* is defined and owned through the the `shared_ptr< Interface>` that will take care of its deallocation when needed...
```
  DagMC *dagmc = new DagMC();
  delete dagmc;
```

